## LAYER 2 — Backend Node.js

### Set `TZ` environment variable at process start

In `app.js` (or the server entry point), set the process timezone as the
very FIRST line before any other `require()`:

```js
process.env.TZ = 'Asia/Kolkata';
```

This ensures `new Date()` on the server always reflects IST, which prevents
accidental UTC offsets when building SQL parameter strings or log entries.

#### ❌ Forbidden — relying on the host OS timezone:
```js
const now = new Date(); // May be UTC or any server timezone
const ts  = Date.now(); // fine for epoch ms — not for wall-clock strings
```

#### ✅ Required — after `process.env.TZ = 'Asia/Kolkata'`:
```js
// All new Date() calls are now IST wall clock on the server
const now = new Date();
const isoIST = now.toISOString(); // still UTC-based ISO, but display will be IST
```

When building SQL timestamp strings explicitly, always include the offset:

```js
const istNow = new Date().toLocaleString('sv-SE', { timeZone: 'Asia/Kolkata' }).replace(' ', 'T');
// → "2026-04-30T21:30:00" (IST wall clock, use for SQL DATETIME2 inserts)
```

